<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tian Heng</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/202-bce">202 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian">Tian</a> Heng (died 202 BCE) was a member of the royal house of the old state of Qi who, after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, made himself and his relatives kings of Qi and fought both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-xin">Han Xin</a> conquered Qi he fled to an island with some 500 followers. Summoned by Liu Bang, now emperor, he killed himself rather than submit, and his followers did the same.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Tian Heng followed his elder brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-rong">Tian Rong</a> through the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> rebellions and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chu-han-contention">Chu–Han Contention</a>. The brothers served several claimants to the throne of Qi before Tian Rong rose and took it himself. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a>, who saw him as a threat to the order he had set up after Qin, attacked Qi and defeated Tian Rong, who was killed, and installed Tian Jia, younger brother of King Tian Jian. Tian Heng's forces drove Tian Jia out, and in 205 BCE he made Tian Rong's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-guang">Tian Guang</a> king.</p><p>In 203 BCE Liu Bang, King of Han, sent his adviser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-yiji">Li Yiji</a> to persuade Qi to submit, and the king agreed. The Han general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-xin">Han Xin</a>, on the advice of Kuai Che, attacked anyway. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-guang">Tian Guang</a> and Tian Heng, enraged by what seemed a ruse, had Li Yiji boiled alive. Han Xin quickly defeated the surprised Qi army and took the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linzi">Linzi</a>; Tian Guang was captured and killed despite help from Chu. Tian Heng proclaimed himself King of Qi and fled to Peng Yue, King of Liang.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a>'s defeat at Gaixia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> made himself emperor, and Tian Heng took his followers to an island. Fearing his influence in Qi, Liu Bang offered a pardon. Tian Heng said he feared the revenge of Li Shang, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-yiji">Li Yiji</a>'s brother and now a Han general; Liu Bang forbade Li Shang on pain of the execution of his family to harm him, while threatening force if Tian Heng did not come at once.</p><p>Tian Heng set out for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> with two retainers. At the carriage station of Shixiang, some thirty li from the city, he told them he could not bear to face north and serve the man who had once been his equal, beside the brother of the man he had boiled, and bade them carry his head to the emperor. He then cut his own throat. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> wept and had him buried with royal honours; the two retainers killed themselves beside the grave, and the followers on the island did the same when they heard. Tian Heng Island, off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimo">Jimo</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-sea">Yellow Sea</a>, is said to be their refuge, though no archaeological evidence confirms it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tian Heng” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Xiang Yu invaded in 205 BCE and Tian Rong was killed, but <b>Tian Heng</b> rallied resistance and installed Tian Rong's son Tian Guang as king, with himself as chancellor.</blockquote><a href="/state/qi-206-bc">Qi (206 BC)</a></li></ul></section>
